---
/**
 * The case for learning one thing a day, and the three ways to keep coming
 * back.
 *
 * Sam's ask, 2026-09-12: a quote encouraging people to learn something a day
 * and the benefits over the long run, then bookmark, subscribe and sign up.
 *
 * THE LINE THAT DECIDES WHAT THIS SAYS. It would be easy to write "learn 1%
 * a day and you will be 37 times better in a year". That number is real
 * arithmetic, 1.01^365, and it is not a real claim about learning: nobody
 * compounds skill at a fixed daily rate, and putting it on the page would be
 * the same kind of unsupported promise the thumbnail tests exist to stop.
 *
 * So the argument here is the honest one, which is also the more persuasive
 * one to an engineer: the value is in the RETURNING, not in a growth rate.
 * Three asks, in the order of how much they cost the reader.
 */
import {CHANNEL_URL} from '../lib/channel';
import YouTubeMark from './YouTubeMark.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<section class="oneaday" aria-labelledby="oneaday-heading">
  <blockquote>
    <p id="oneaday-heading">
      One thing a day is not a small amount of learning. It is the only amount
      that survives a busy week.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p class="oneaday-lede">
    Nobody becomes a senior engineer in a weekend of study, and nobody stays one
    by stopping. What actually separates engineers over a few years is not how
    hard they pushed in any one month. It is whether they kept turning up at
    all.
  </p>

  <p>
    An hour a day for a year is more time than most people spend on any course
    they ever pay for, and it costs nothing except the decision to come back
    tomorrow. Twenty minutes a day still gets you there, more slowly and far
    more reliably, because twenty minutes survives the weeks when everything is
    on fire and an hour does not.
  </p>

  <p>
    The compounding is real, but it is not arithmetic. It is that each thing you
    understand makes the next thing cheaper to understand. The first time you
    read about consumer rebalancing it is new. By the time you meet it again in
    a different system, it is a variation on something you already know, and you
    learn it in a tenth of the time.
  </p>

  <h3>Three ways to keep coming back</h3>
  <ol class="oneaday-asks">
    <li>
      <strong>Bookmark this page.</strong> Costs nothing and takes a second.
      <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd>, or <kbd>Cmd</kbd> + <kbd>D</kbd> on a Mac.
      One new thing is added here most weeks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subscribe on YouTube.</strong> Every article here has a video, and
      subscribing is how the next one finds you rather than the other way round.
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={CHANNEL_URL} rel="noopener">
        <YouTubeMark size={16} /> Subscribe on YouTube
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Get it by email.</strong> If you would rather not rely on an
      algorithm deciding what you see, the newsletter sends new work as it
      lands. <a href="#signup">Sign up below</a>, one click to leave, always.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="oneaday-close">
    If you only do one of these, make it the bookmark. It is the one that
    depends on nobody but you.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .oneaday {
    margin: 3.5rem 0;
    padding: 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline, #e4e4e0);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }

  .oneaday blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0 0 0 1.1rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent, #d93a3a);
  }

  .oneaday blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .oneaday-lede {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .oneaday h3 {
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .oneaday-asks {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }

  .oneaday-asks .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
  }

  .oneaday kbd {
    padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--hairline, #d4d4d0);
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    font-family: inherit;
  }

  .oneaday-close {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft, #5b6472);
  }
</style>
